$r = (RUN 'SHOW_IT {"subject": "Two panels side by side on one image, divided down the middle. LEFT PANEL: the tattoo design alone on a plain white background, shown flat exactly as described, no body, drawn large so it fills most of the panel. RIGHT PANEL: Studio photograph of a real person''s body showing the same exact tattoo where described, healed tattoo on real skin in soft even light, the rest of the body modestly covered by simple plain clothing, only the tattooed area bare, plain seamless light grey studio backdrop - no room, no furniture, no scenery. The tattoo in both panels is identical. The tattoo: A childhood house sits at the bottom of an enormous ancient tree whose roots descend beneath it like an underground second world. Small fragments of childhood are almost hidden inside the root system rather than arranged around the house as symbols. Origin, mysterious, grounded, cinematic. In the right panel it is on a man, outer upper arm flowing onto shoulder. Create exceptional contemporary tattoo artwork inspired by a human life experience. PERSONAL STORY IS NOT CLIP ART AND NOT A SCRAPBOOK. The finished piece must be desirable as artwork even if the viewer never learns the story behind it. Build one strong visual concept rather than surrounding a central object with collections of explanatory symbols. Avoid the visual language of greeting cards, inspirational posters, stock memorial art, generic Pinterest tattoos and sentimental family collages. Do not automatically add flowers, clocks, compasses, angel wings, hearts, infinity symbols, coordinates, dates, handwriting, banners, footprints, birds, fishing poles or other conventional memory symbols unless that element is essential to the specific artistic concept. Do not create an isolated object floating on skin with decorative filler around it. Think like an exceptional tattoo artist, illustrator, painter, cinematographer and visual storyteller. Use composition, scale, perspective, anatomy, light, atmosphere, negative space, motion, environment, texture and visual contradiction to communicate emotion. The tattoo may use Realism, Black and Grey Realism, Colour Realism, Illustrative, Fine Line, Sketch, Blackwork, Dotwork, Watercolour, Neo-Traditional, New School, surrealism, engraving, painterly techniques or intelligent combinations when appropriate. Allow beautiful work, dark work, strange work, sensual work, funny work, painful work, quiet work and spectacular work. Humor must come from the situation or visual idea rather than cartoon clip art. Surrealism must feel intentional and artistically resolved rather than random objects combined together. When depicting people, use convincing anatomy, gesture, expression and body language. When depicting environments, create real depth and atmosphere rather than flat scenery. When using a mundane object, elevate it through composition, light, scale, transformation or context. Preserve areas of skin and negative space when they strengthen readability. Design specifically for tattooing and for the stated body placement, allowing the composition to follow muscle, bone, curvature and viewing angle. Large concepts should earn their scale through visual richness rather than simply enlarging an icon. Small concepts should remain visually powerful and legible. Do not make every concept sentimental. Do not make every concept dark. Do not make every concept pretty. Do not make every concept safe. Do not make every concept literal. The customers real details will come later through Aura. This example only demonstrates what extraordinary art can grow from an ordinary human experience. MY LIFE GAVE ME THE IDEA. ART TURNED IT INTO THE TATTOO. PRESENTATION FORMAT Generate one finished presentation image containing two coordinated panels. LEFT PANEL: Show the complete tattoo artwork cleanly on a simple neutral background. This is the actual tattoo design. RIGHT PANEL: Show the EXACT SAME tattoo artwork realistically tattooed on the stated body placement. Preserve the same subject, composition, orientation, proportions, important details and colors. Do not reinterpret or redesign the artwork for the body panel. Adapt only what is physically necessary for natural curvature and perspective. The tattoo must appear genuinely embedded in skin with realistic scale and integration rather than pasted onto a photograph. The body should support the tattoo rather than dominate the image. Frame enough anatomy to make the placement immediately understandable. Use varied adult men and women across the collection and varied skin tones where appropriate. Clothing should naturally expose the specified tattoo area without distracting from the artwork. LEFT IS THE ART. RIGHT IS THE SAME ART WORN ON THE BODY. The goal of every image is: THAT IS NOT MY STORY. BUT I WANT MY STORY TURNED INTO ART LIKE THAT. The tattoo in both panels is identical.", "raw": true, "aspect": "3:2"}' | Out-String)
