"== Pin-Up Girls #01 =="
$r = (RUN 'SHOW_IT {"subject": "Two panels side by side on one image, divided down the middle. LEFT PANEL: the tattoo design alone on a plain white background, shown flat exactly as described, no body, drawn large so it fills most of the panel. RIGHT PANEL: Studio photograph of a real person''s body showing the same exact tattoo where described, healed tattoo on real skin in soft even light, the rest of the body modestly covered by simple plain clothing, only the tattooed area bare, plain seamless light grey studio backdrop - no room, no furniture, no scenery. The tattoo in both panels is identical. The tattoo: A classic sailor woman sits confidently on an enormous ship anchor, but the treatment is elevated with sweeping windblown hair, rolling ocean and dramatic sunset light rather than simple vintage flash. Classic, confident, nautical, iconic. In the right panel it is on a man, outer upper arm. Create exceptional contemporary tattoo artwork centered on an adult woman with unmistakable personality, presence and visual power. THE WOMAN IS THE HERO. Do not treat Pin-Up Girls as a collection of vintage costumes, occupations, props or generic sexual poses. Do not create 100 variations of the same 1940s woman. Do not default to red lipstick, victory rolls, tiny waists, exaggerated breasts, high heels, roses and one hand resting on the hip. Do not automatically make the woman smile. Do not automatically make her look seductively toward the viewer. Do not use sexualization as a substitute for character. Beauty, sensuality and glamour are welcome, but personality and artistic presence are more important. Every woman must feel like a character rather than a mannequin. Use facial expression, posture, gesture, movement, fashion, lighting, environment, perspective and composition to create personality. Rotate full figures, three-quarter figures, waist-up compositions, portraits, profiles, close-ups, reclining poses, standing poses, sitting poses, walking figures, figures in motion, over-the-shoulder views and unusual perspectives. Use a broad range of adult women with naturally varied body types, skin tones, facial structures, hair textures, hairstyles and aesthetics. Do not make representation feel forced or tokenized. Simply create a believable world filled with different extraordinary women. The artistic treatment may draw from American Traditional, Neo-Traditional, Realism, Black and Grey Realism, Colour Realism, Fine Line, Chicano-influenced tattooing, Japanese-influenced composition, Illustrative, Watercolour, Dotwork, New School, Gothic, Art Nouveau, surrealism, fashion illustration, cinematic realism and intelligent hybrids. Maintain strong tattoo readability. Use confident silhouettes, controlled detail, intentional negative space, clear focal hierarchy and compositions capable of aging as tattoos. The environment may support the woman but must never overwhelm her. Cars, motorcycles, horses, dragons, animals, architecture, flowers, weapons, machines and landscapes are supporting elements. THE WOMAN REMAINS THE HERO. Allow the category to move freely between classic, contemporary, beautiful, glamorous, athletic, rebellious, dark, Gothic, mythological, futuristic, surreal and completely impossible worlds. Some pieces should feel timeless. Some should feel unmistakably contemporary. Some should be beautiful. Some should be sexy. Some should be elegant. Some should be dangerous. Some should be dark. Some should be powerful. Some should be strange. Some should be spectacular. Do not make everything pretty. Do not make everything dark. Do not make everything vintage. Do not make everything sexual. Do not make everything safe. ARTISTIC AMBITION IS MORE IMPORTANT THAN NOVELTY. A strange concept must still become exceptional artwork rather than a joke. When the concept is humorous or impossible, render it with enough artistic conviction that someone would genuinely want the tattoo. Design the womans pose specifically for the stated body placement. A reclining woman may sweep across a thigh. A tall standing figure may follow the calf. Hair may wrap naturally around the shoulder. A portrait may dominate the upper arm. A long silhouette may travel vertically along the ribs. Fabric, water, smoke, hair and environmental elements may help the composition follow anatomy. DO NOT DESIGN A PIN-UP GIRL AND THEN FIND SOMEWHERE TO PUT HER. DESIGN HER FOR THE BODY SHE WILL LIVE ON. PRESENTATION FORMAT Every generated image contains two coordinated panels. LEFT PANEL: Show the complete finished tattoo artwork cleanly on a simple neutral background. This is the actual tattoo design. RIGHT PANEL: Show the EXACT SAME artwork realistically tattooed on the specified body placement. The woman must remain identical. Preserve: Face Hair Pose Body Expression Clothing Supporting elements Composition Color Lighting Orientation Major details Do not redesign the tattoo when placing it on the body. Adapt only what is physically necessary for realistic curvature, perspective and skin integration. The tattoo must look genuinely embedded in skin rather than pasted onto a photograph. Frame enough of the human body to make placement immediately understandable without allowing the model to dominate the tattoo. Use varied adult models naturally across the collection. LEFT IS THE ART. RIGHT IS THE SAME ART WORN ON THE BODY. The tattoo in both panels is identical.", "raw": true, "aspect": "3:2"}' | Out-String)
